  * The GPU shader cache (/var/tmp/.../m2v) needs a writable file system with free space, but the
  * root volume is deliberately not grown (a grown volume makes the iOS data migration start and
  * never finish). Instead deploy writes a second, small empty HFS+ volume into a pinned userdata
- * region; sweet_ufs exposes it as /dev/ufscache0 (0272) and this file mounts it at
- * /private/var/tmp before launchd starts.
+ * region; sweet_ufs exposes it as /dev/ufscache0 (0272) and this file mounts it before launchd starts.
+ *
+ * 0350: the volume is mounted at /private/var, not /private/var/tmp. On this port the root volume
+ * starts at 96.8 % used and /private/var is nearly empty in the image, so the whole data volume is
+ * built at run time: measured across three boots it grew by ~204 MiB (db/uuidtext 116 MiB,
+ * db/diagnostics 7 MiB, mobile/Library 58 MiB, log 5 MiB, Keychains 3 MiB, containers 3 MiB, ...)
+ * and pushed the root to 99.1 % full. At that fill HFS+ can no longer grow its metadata reliably,
+ * so securityd failed to open its keychain database (EMFILE + ENOSPC in the same millisecond),
+ * ~80 user-session daemons died in libdispatch at 42.48 s and SpringBoard never drew the lock
+ * screen. Moving the data volume onto the 2 GiB cache volume keeps the root at its static fill;
+ * the empty cache volume then needs the directory skeleton the image used to provide, which
+ * cv_seed_skeleton() recreates (170 directories taken from the image, parents first).
  *
  * Boot-arg pandora_cachevol=1 enables it (default off, so a bad volume can never stop the boot).
+ * Boot-arg pandora_varcache=0 keeps the mount but skips the skeleton, for a bisect.
  */
